This project ports aiming, smoothing, and cursor-follow ideas from:

  WatchPointer
  Copyright (c) 2026 Umar Afzal
  https://github.com/umarz317/WatchPointer
  Licensed under the MIT License, reproduced below.

Ported ideas (reimplemented in Kotlin and Rust, not copied as Swift source):

  - Sample gravity and gyroscope at 100 Hz.
  - Elevation from the gravity vector, so pitch does not integrate drift.
  - Heading by integrating rotation about the vertical axis.
  - Project that aim onto the screen like a laser on a flat wall.
  - Smooth samples with a 1€ filter (Casiez, Roussel, Vogel, CHI 2012)
    driven by the watch timestamps, not by Bluetooth arrival time.
  - Move the desktop cursor on its own 240 Hz loop.
  - Clicks and drags land where the pointer was when the finger went down.
  - Hold-to-drag threshold of 0.35 s.

The 1€ filter algorithm is from:

  Géry Casiez, Nicolas Roussel, Daniel Vogel. "1€ Filter: A Simple Speed-based
  Low-pass Filter for Noisy Input in Interactive Systems". CHI 2012.
  https://gery.casiez.net/1euro/

wear-pointer uses its own packet layout (version, sequence, microsecond
timestamp) and, by default, the opposite Bluetooth role: the watch is the
GATT peripheral and the desktop is the central. The WatchPointer direction
(desktop advertises, watch connects) is the documented fallback. See
docs/fallback-transport.md and protocol/SPEC.md.

The wlr-virtual-pointer-unstable-v1 protocol used by the Hyprland backend is
Copyright © 2019 Josef Gajdusek and is MIT-licensed. This repository does not
vendor that XML; the receiver links the wayland-protocols-wlr crate.

